  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/89b0be0ca7ae1a6e76d32bfc669d6f554deaaad2055b2875e3f6a49821043205.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?",
  "worked_solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/cfb095836b7498c91a5cbd9d2cb694715a0d89a12a5e6ea5ff417342c4bde798.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph III shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
